    {
      "id": "s2023-final-q09",
      "number": 9,
      "points": 4,
      "stem": "Using the 2nd order Taylor polynomial of $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x}$ centered at $a = 3$,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\pi} \\approx \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{9}(\\pi - 3) + \\frac{2}{27}(\\pi - 3)^2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the Taylor Remainder Theorem, this approximation has error equal to",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "$-\\frac{(\\pi - 3)^3}{6c^4}$ for some $c$ between 3 and $\\pi$"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "$-\\frac{(\\pi - 3)^3}{3c^3}$ for some $c$ between 3 and $\\pi$"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "$-\\frac{(\\pi - 3)^3}{3c^4}$ for some $c$ between 3 and $\\pi$"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "$-\\frac{(\\pi - 3)^3}{6c^3}$ for some $c$ between 3 and $\\pi$"
        },
        {
          "label": "E",
          "text": "$-\\frac{(\\pi - 3)^3}{c^4}$ for some $c$ between 3 and $\\pi$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "E",
      "answerSource": "derived",
      "lessonIds": [
        "L28"
      ],
      "sourcePage": 6,
      "solution": "**Note on this question:** The printed coefficient $\\frac{2}{27}$ should be $\\frac{1}{27}$, since $\\frac{f''(3)}{2!}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{2}{27}=\\frac{1}{27}$, so the printed expression is not exactly the 2nd order Taylor polynomial the stem names. Choice E is the remainder theorem's form for that polynomial: $\\frac{f'''(c)}{3!}(\\pi-3)^3$ with $f'''(c)=-\\frac{6}{c^4}$ gives $-\\frac{(\\pi-3)^3}{c^4}$.",
      "status": "reviewed"
    },
